                "event": "Flood Watch",
                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Anchorage AK",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued September 24 at 1:57PM AKDT until September 26 at 1:00PM AKDT by NWS Anchorage AK",
                "description": "* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall is possible.\n\n* WHERE...Interior Kuskokwim Delta, including Kwethluk, Kasigluk,\nBethel, Napaskiak, Napakiak, Oscarville, and other nearby villages.\n\n* WHEN...Through Saturday afternoon.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive rain in combination of persistent high water\nlevels and storm surge may result in flooding of rivers, creeks,\nstreams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations. Creeks and\nstreams may rise out of their banks. Low-water crossings may be\nflooded. Area creeks and streams are running high and could flood\nwith more heavy rain.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- A strong storm moving into the eastern Bering Sea will bring\nheavy rainfall and storm surge to the area. The Kuskokwim River\nis currently running high, with statistics showing the river is\nalready above the 90th percentile in flow for this time of year.\n1 to 2 inches of rainfall across the Kilbuck Mountains, as well\nas across the lower Kuskokwim, is likely to exacerbate high water\nlevels for the Kwethluk River, Johnson River / tundra villages,\nas well as other smaller tributaries and sloughs of the\nKuskokwim.\n\nConfidence is moderate-to-high that flooding of low-lying areas\nwill occur for Kwethluk and Kasigluk, where high-water was\nreported last week. Confidence is lower for flooding elsewhere\nand will be dependent on the rainfall amount and degree of\ncompounding effects from coastal surge and tide cycle.\n\n- Http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood",
                "instruction": "Stay tuned to further developments by listening to your local radio,\ntelevision, or NOAA Weather Radio for further information.\n\nYou should monitor later forecasts and be alert for possible Flood\nWarnings. Those living in areas prone to flooding should be prepared\nto take action should flooding develop.",
                "response": "Prepare",
                "note": null,
